## Example Usage

{{tffile "examples/backup/restore/main.tf"}}
{{tffile "examples/backup/restore/variables.tf"}}

The restore operation is asynchronous. Terraform waits for per-resource statuses to reach a terminal state and **fails apply if any affected resource reports `error`**. Reasons returned by the API are available in `resources_reason`. Before creating a restore, the provider checks for an existing restore with the same checkpoint and parameters and reuses it, preventing duplicate requests after a failed apply. Re-adding identical configuration therefore adopts the existing restore; change `restore_name` when a distinct restore is intended. This resource tracks the restore operation, not the resulting VM: removing it from configuration only removes operation state. To manage the restored VM, set `vkcs_compute_instance.restore_id` to this resource's `id`; the compute resource adopts the resulting Nova instance and deletes that VM when destroyed. Changing any restore input starts a new restore operation. Database restore is not supported by this resource.

